meeting with the prime minister today  what do you think it could achieve  well what the prime minister wants to do  is to listen to fans listen to those  people who have a real  interest in football and are passionate  about football  and outline his clear view that this is  a super  league that must be stopped because it's  just simply not fair  it's not right football is rooted in the  communities and  clubs have grown up in and um you know  it's there for the fans it's there to be  uh you know  part of that community and this super  league looks as if it would  really destroy that but also the key  core element of football which is  competition  because as we uh watch the premiership  it's not  just a battle for the top it's also a  battle for survival at the bottom  and this seems to have been dreamed up  by  money men as against something for  football fans and that's why  the prime minister is so opposed to it  and yeah yeah you will have heard in our  report that fiontino perez  saying that this is about saving  football  well in fairness he was bound to say  that wasn't he  and um what we are wanting to do is to  put the interests of  fans right at the heart of this but the  interests of a  whole uh game of football at the heart  of this and that's not something that  these proposals for a european super  league is doing  uh it's ignoring communities it's  ignoring  uh the competition that exists in the  game of football  and that's why the culture secretary  yesterday  and the house of commons made it  absolutely clear the steps that the  government is going to be taking  to ensure that uh this is stopped we're  going to be putting  uh all our support behind the football  association and uefa  in terms of their steps that they may  need to take but if they're not  successful in doing that  the culture secretary has been  absolutely clear that we won't hesitate  to take  further steps in order to ensure that  this european super league  won't go ahead well he wasn't clear  though was he when he's  speaking yesterday oliver dowden said  the government will do whatever it takes  to stop the league  well what can the government actually do  well um oliver's uh dowden the culture  secretary  set out a number of steps the first step  of that is actually working with the faa  and uefa  in order to find a solution within the  football family and obviously that's the  preferred option  and we'd hope that these clubs step back  from the proposals that they have put  forward  if that's something that isn't going to  happen um  the culture secretary made it absolutely  clear in the house of commons that  we'd look at every single option in  order to stop this moving forward and  we are we're focusing uh on the initial  stages in supporting bfa  supporting uefa to stop this going ahead  but you cannot be clearer  the culture secretary couldn't have been  clearer about the government's  commitment to ensure that this doesn't  proceed  including the announcement of uh tracy  crouch leading the fan-based review  yeah but with all due respect i mean  look a lot of football fans will be  pleased to hear you say what you've said  but it doesn't  it still leaves that very big question  what in reality can the government do  yes you you you've got the tracy crouch  review yes you're talking to  the fa and fans representatives today  but in actual solid terms  you know these these are ultimately  private businesses can you  actually intervene in any way  well the government of course can  intervene and the government has made it  clear that we  would be willing to intervene if a  solution isn't being able to be found  between vfa and uefa well our first  initial efforts have got to be  supporting the faa and supporting uefa  to find a solution to this and make sure  that the  european super league doesn't go ahead  but if that  doesn't uh come about if we're not able  to stop it through those means  the government reserves its position in  order to take further action  in order to stop this european super  league it's just simply not right for  football  football is about bringing people  together yeah it's the first give me  foreign absolutely will take action  such as well the government reserves  this position to take any action that's  required  including the need to take legislation  the need to take  sanctions in order to ensure that we  protect football interests in this  country and that's the right approach to  do  but the the first most important step is  to ensure  that we put our full support behind the  football association who is equally  opposed to this and we saw  uh prince prince william voicing his  concerns uh  last night about this and uh the  government is right to be fully  supporting the fa  in its opposition and trying to find a  solution to this  what we would encourage is all of these  six clubs to step back  it's not in the spirit of football it's  not what the  um it's not what fans want you've heard  it it's not what players want it's not  what managers want  um it seems to be what the money men  want and that isn't what should be  driving football  okay so what sort of sanctions are  available to government  well a whole range of sanctions but as i  stated the first step is  to support vfa and support uefa uh in  order to be able to  give you i mean minister with the  greatest of respect and i apologize for  for interrupting  um you've you've said what the first  steps are we're clear on that you said  there are  you know a range of sanctions available  again such as  i mean what realistically can you do  well the government is willing to sort  of review all aspects it's willing to be  able to look at  what further sanctions and impositions  that he can put on clubs  it could look at legislative options as  well but  we focus very much on the first stage  and the first step but  in reserve there is the option for the  government to take further action  and of course we have got the fan base  review that uh tracy crouch is going to  be leading  there's going to be making sure that we  take the steps that are required  in order to be able to protect the  interests of fans  in football can we have a look at the  the covid situation  if you would obviously we had the news  yesterday that india's going on to the  red list  already what we think about 303 known  cases of the indian variant  in the uk which is a double mutation  which causes some concern  um have we left it too long to put india  on the red list  and and if not why are we leaving it  till four o'clock  friday morning before that actually  happens  well it's as you all know it's standard  practice to give people a sort of short  window in order to be able to  uh manage their affairs it's the right  approach to do it's the approach that we  take them with other countries around  the world when they've gone on to the  red list  the government continuously reviews the  data  continues continuously reviews the  information that we're getting from the  scientific community  um in terms of what countries should be  entering  being put onto the red list and sadly  india has been one of those countries  that's had to be added  i mean how concerned are you and your  colleagues and sage as far as you know  about that about the indian variant and  and the effectiveness of our vaccine  program against it  well we've seen an enormous success in  terms of our vaccine program  leading the world in the rolled out of  our  vaccines whether that's the oxford  astrazeneca  fisa vaccine right across the country  and it gives us a level of protection  that few and other countries are  able to sort of rival but we have to  remain vigilant we have to remain  vigilant against new variants  that's why we've had to place india on  that red list  and it's the fact that we always take  these matters seriously that we've taken  the action and the steps to place  india onto that red list i mean there 